# sdkconfig.defaults — ESP32-S3 target defaults (S15 slice a).
#
# DRIFT RULE (added S14/S15 Amendments, 2026-09-02): a generated
# `sdkconfig` in this directory (untracked — never commit it) does NOT
# pick up new entries added here after it first exists. Kconfig only
# fills in a value from *.defaults for a key that is ABSENT from
# sdkconfig; a key already present — even written as
# "# CONFIG_FOO is not set" — is left exactly as it is. Running
# `idf.py reconfigure` after editing this file is NOT enough to make an
# existing checkout pick up a new default: the key must either be
# deleted from the board's sdkconfig (so Kconfig re-derives it from here)
# or the whole sdkconfig regenerated (`idf.py fullclean` then
# reconfigure). Live example: CONFIG_FREERTOS_PLACE_FUNCTIONS_INTO_FLASH=y
# was added to this file in PR #98, but it never reached the maintainer's
# already-generated board sdkconfig — that board is still running without
# it today, silently, because the key was already present there (unset)
# from an earlier configure. When adding a default that must actually
# take effect on an existing board, say so explicitly in the PR body and
# tell the maintainer which key(s) to delete from their local sdkconfig
# (never edit that file from CI or an agent branch — it is untracked and
# board-specific).
#
# Board: Waveshare ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-1.46 (arrives the day after this
# slice lands — these values are Waveshare's published module spec,
# NOT yet confirmed against the physical board). Waveshare's product page
# for this SKU specifies an ESP32-S3R8 module: 16 MB flash, 8 MB **octal**
# PSRAM (the "R8" suffix in Espressif's own naming is octal SPI PSRAM,
# distinct from "R2"/quad). If the board that arrives reports otherwise
# (`idf.py build` fails to boot / `esp_psram` init fails, or `esptool.py
# flash_id` shows a different flash size), flip CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_* and
# CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_* below and re-flash — nothing else in this
# slice depends on the exact size.
#
# PSRAM is required, not optional: fp_pack_t (festpack/include/fp_pack.h)
# budgets ~48 KB and ff_shell_t (app/include/ff_shell.h) budgets 22 KB —
# comfortably inside ESP32-S3's ~512 KB internal SRAM on their own, but
# S15 slices b-e add the LVGL v9 display driver, its frame buffer(s) at
# 412x412, and the SPD2010 QSPI panel/touch driver on top, which is the
# actual reason PSRAM has to be on from this slice onward rather than
# added later — turning it on after buffers are already sized against
# internal SRAM would mean re-tuning those buffers too.

CONFIG_IDF_TARGET="esp32s3"

# --- PSRAM -------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_SPIRAM=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_OCT=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_SPEED_80M=y
# Map PSRAM into the regular malloc() pool too, not just the explicit
# EXT_RAM_BSS_ATTR placement fp_pack.h's header comment mentions — belt
# and suspenders until slice b/d actually need to place bytes deliberately.
CONFIG_SPIRAM_USE_MALLOC=y

# --- Flash ---------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_16MB=y
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_SINGLE_APP_LARGE=y

# --- Misc ------------------------------------------------------------------
# This slice boots to Radar's no-fix state with no peripherals touched —
# no display, no UART, no sensors (those are slices b/c/d). Leave the
# console on the default UART so `idf.py monitor` shows app_main's boot
# log tomorrow.
CONFIG_ESP_MAIN_TASK_STACK_SIZE=8192

# --- LVGL v9 (S15 slice b) -------------------------------------------------
# The device's LVGL config comes from Kconfig (the esp registry lvgl
# component reads CONFIG_LV_* rather than firmware/lv_conf.h, which is the
# SIM's config). These values mirror the non-default settings the sim's
# lv_conf.h sets, so the SAME app/screens/*.c render the same pixels:
#   - RGB565 (16-bit) to match the SPD2010 panel (the sim renders 32-bit
#     ARGB for SDL, but pixel *layout* the screens produce is identical;
#     the panel just wants 16bpp — esp_lvgl_port swap_bytes handles the
#     big-endian wire order, so LV_COLOR_16_SWAP stays OFF to avoid a
#     double swap).
#   - the exact Montserrat sizes ff_theme.h references (14/16/20/22/24/36),
#     default 24, matching lv_conf.h lines 32-43.
#   - TILEVIEW (scr_nav's three-face swipe shell) + ARC (radar rings).
CONFIG_LV_COLOR_DEPTH_16=y
CONFIG_LV_FONT_MONTSERRAT_14=y
CONFIG_LV_FONT_MONTSERRAT_16=y
CONFIG_LV_FONT_MONTSERRAT_20=y
CONFIG_LV_FONT_MONTSERRAT_22=y
CONFIG_LV_FONT_MONTSERRAT_24=y
CONFIG_LV_FONT_MONTSERRAT_36=y
CONFIG_LV_FONT_DEFAULT_MONTSERRAT_24=y
CONFIG_LV_USE_TILEVIEW=y
CONFIG_LV_USE_ARC=y
# LVGL object heap — the radar face's static point pools are light, but
# give the widget tree comfortable headroom (default is smaller).
CONFIG_LV_MEM_SIZE_KILOBYTES=64
# Espressif's registry lvgl component defaults CONFIG_LV_BUILD_EXAMPLES and
# CONFIG_LV_BUILD_DEMOS to y — LVGL's own examples/ and demos/ trees get
# compiled into the app image even though this project never calls
# lv_example_*() or lv_demo_*() anywhere; nothing here references them.
# Confirmed against the maintainer's real board sdkconfig (both =y there,
# left over from the component's own default — the drift rule above is
# exactly why: see this file's header) — 258 of that build's 1910 linked
# objects are LVGL example/demo code with zero callers. Off here so a
# fresh checkout doesn't carry that dead weight into flash budget or link
# time. The sim's CMakeLists.txt already forces the equivalent
# CONFIG_LV_BUILD_DEMOS/CONFIG_LV_BUILD_EXAMPLES OFF for FetchContent's
# lvgl — this brings the device build's default in line with the sim's.
CONFIG_LV_BUILD_EXAMPLES=n
CONFIG_LV_BUILD_DEMOS=n

# --- RAM headroom (S15 slice b) --------------------------------------------
# On the IRAM "99.99%" concern S15a raised: measured, that 16 KB IRAM region
# holds IDF's forced-IRAM interrupt/panic/vector code and is a FIXED baseline
# — it read 16383/16384 in S15a with zero drivers, and reads exactly the same
# now with the SPD2010 panel + LVGL + touch linked, because ALL of that driver
# code lands in Flash Code + DIRAM, none in IRAM. So the driver does not, and
# cannot, push that region; it is not a real constraint for this slice.
# The RAM that DOES matter (DIRAM / internal SRAM, shared with LVGL objects,
# task stacks, .bss/.data) has ~240 KB free. This option moves non-ISR
# FreeRTOS functions from DIRAM .text to flash (~9 KB reclaimed) for extra
# DIRAM headroom before slices c/d/e pile on. Behaviourally transparent in
# normal operation (only affects code that runs with the flash cache
# disabled, which this app does not do).
CONFIG_FREERTOS_PLACE_FUNCTIONS_INTO_FLASH=y


# --- Console: USB-Serial/JTAG only, NOT UART0 (S15c amendment) -------------
# S15c's UART transport claims GPIO43/44 for the mesh link to the comms
# brain. ESP-IDF's stock default for this target keeps UART0 (whose OWN
# default TX/RX pins are ALSO GPIO43/44) as the PRIMARY console, with
# USB-Serial/JTAG only as a SECONDARY mirror (esp-idf's own
# components/esp_system/Kconfig ESP_CONSOLE_UART/ESP_CONSOLE_SECONDARY
# choices) — meaning a fresh checkout's boot log would keep printing onto
# the same physical pins the comms brain listens on. Flipped here so
# USB-Serial/JTAG is the ONLY console (primary, secondary NONE): no boot
# log ever reaches GPIO43/44 on a fresh checkout, whether or not
# CONFIG_FF_LINK is set to UART.
#
# This does NOT fully silence GPIO43/44 at every point in boot — see
# docs/specs/S15-esp32s3-target.md's Amendments ("boot-garbage window")
# for the one thing this setting cannot reach: the ROM's own first-stage
# boot banner ("ets ... rst:0x1 (POWERON)..."), which is hardware-fixed to
# UART0's default pins and prints BEFORE any flash code — bootloader or
# app — has read a single byte of Kconfig. This setting DOES cover the
# second-stage bootloader's own log (bootloader_support/src/
# bootloader_console.c reads this SAME CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_* namespace) and
# every app-level ESP_LOGx call — both moved off UART0 entirely.
#
# DRIFT-RULE WARNING (this file's own header, and PR #172): a generated
# `sdkconfig` that ALREADY EXISTS (the maintainer's real board file,
# untracked) does NOT pick up this new default — Kconfig only fills in a
# key that is ABSENT from an existing sdkconfig, and CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_*
# is very much already present there (as CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_UART_DEFAULT=y
# et al.). The maintainer must delete those CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_* keys from
# the board's sdkconfig (or `idf.py fullclean` + reconfigure it entirely)
# for this new default to actually take effect on real hardware — see the
# S15 Amendment for the full list of keys and the accompanying
# CONFIG_FF_LINK_UART=y the maintainer must also set by hand there (a
# brand-new key, unlike CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_*, so it WOULD pick up this
# file's FF_LINK_NONE default silently otherwise — no drift-rule exception
# for a key that never existed before).
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_UART_DEFAULT=n
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG=y
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_SECONDARY_NONE=y

# --- Task watchdog (TWDT), 2026-09-08 QA hardening item 1b -----------------
# Previously ABSENT from this file entirely — a fresh checkout got
# whatever ESP-IDF's own Kconfig defaults happen to be (TWDT on, 5s
# timeout, idle tasks only, panic OFF) purely by luck, not by this
# project's own choice. Made explicit here and reconfigured at runtime to
# match (app_main.c's `ff_configure_task_watchdog`, which is the actual
# authority on the timeout value — see that function's own doc comment;
# these keys exist so TWDT is at least ON, with a sane starting timeout,
# before app_main.c's own reconfigure call runs, and so `idf.py
# menuconfig` shows the real picture instead of an invisible IDF default).
#   - CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_PANIC intentionally NOT set here (stays at
#     ESP-IDF's own Kconfig default, off) — a reboot-on-hang policy is a
#     real tradeoff (a frozen puck in the field vs. an unexpected reboot
#     from a slow-but-legitimate operation) that wants the maintainer's
#     own sign-off rather than a default flipped silently underneath
#     them. app_main.c's `ff_configure_task_watchdog` matches this at
#     runtime (`trigger_panic = false`) so the watchdog still logs a trip
#     (task names + a stack dump) but does not reset the device. The
#     opt-in reboot-on-trip behavior lives on branch
#     `qa/wdt-panic-optin`, a one-commit PR against main, for the
#     maintainer to merge if/when they want it.
#   - CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_TIMEOUT_S=15 matches app_main.c's
#     FF_TASK_WDT_TIMEOUT_S; kept in sync by convention, not by any
#     mechanical link between the two — a future change to one should
#     update the other (see FF_TASK_WDT_TIMEOUT_S's own doc comment).
#   - CHECK_IDLE_TASK_CPU0/1=y: unchanged from ESP-IDF's own default,
#     stated explicitly rather than left implicit.
#
# DRIFT-RULE WARNING (this file's own header, and PR #172): the
# maintainer's real board sdkconfig already has every CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_*
# key present (CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_TIMEOUT_S=5, CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_PANIC
# unset) from an earlier configure — this new default will NOT reach that
# board until those keys are deleted from it (or the whole file is
# regenerated) and `idf.py reconfigure` run. Until then the board keeps
# running with a 5s, non-panicking watchdog from Kconfig's OWN default —
# app_main.c's own `esp_task_wdt_reconfigure()` call overrides the
# TIMEOUT_S value at runtime regardless of what's in sdkconfig, so this
# drift only matters for the WINDOW between boot and that call (a few
# hundred ms at most) and for `idf.py menuconfig`'s displayed value.
CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_EN=y
CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_INIT=y
CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_TIMEOUT_S=15
CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_CHECK_IDLE_TASK_CPU0=y
CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_CHECK_IDLE_TASK_CPU1=y

# --- FreeRTOS trace facility, 2026-09-08 QA hardening item 2 ---------------
# Previously off (ESP-IDF's own Kconfig default). Required for
# `uxTaskGetSystemState()` — the only FreeRTOS API that ENUMERATES every
# task — which the new `perf` bench console command (app_main.c,
# `dbgconsole_perf`) uses to report every task's stack high-water mark,
# per this hardening pass's own brief ("task stack high-water marks for
# every task"). Small, fixed per-task RAM cost (each TCB grows by a
# handful of bytes for the extra bookkeeping this enables) — not
# reference-counted or measured separately in this pass; if DIRAM ever
# gets tight enough to matter, `idf.py size-components` will show it
# under the FreeRTOS component's own growth.
#
# DRIFT-RULE CORRECTION (2026-09-08, gate re-run for #239): this pass's
# own earlier diff analysis claimed the maintainer's real board sdkconfig
# was missing this key entirely and would pick up this default for free.
# That was wrong — the board's sdkconfig already carries
# "# CONFIG_FREERTOS_USE_TRACE_FACILITY is not set" explicitly (confirmed
# against the real file, not re-derived), which the drift rule (this
# file's own header) means Kconfig will NOT overwrite with this new
# default. Left as-is on the real board, `perf`'s stack high-water-mark
# reporting FAILS TO LINK — `uxTaskGetSystemState` is compiled out
# entirely when the trace facility is off, not merely a no-op — so this
# is a hard build break, not a degraded-but-booting gap. The maintainer
# must delete the `CONFIG_FREERTOS_USE_TRACE_FACILITY` line from their
# local sdkconfig (same mechanism as the ESP_TASK_WDT_* / STACK_CHECK_*
# keys above/below) and run `idf.py reconfigure` before `perf` will build
# against the real board.
CONFIG_FREERTOS_USE_TRACE_FACILITY=y

# --- Stack smashing protection, 2026-09-08 QA hardening item 4 -------------
# Previously entirely OFF, and DELIBERATELY so in the sense that
# CONFIG_COMPILER_STACK_CHECK_MODE_NONE=y is explicitly PRESENT (not
# merely defaulted) on the maintainer's real board sdkconfig — i.e. this
# is not a "nobody thought about it" gap, it is an existing explicit
# setting this PR proposes changing. NORM ("emit a guard variable for
# functions with vulnerable objects — arrays, large local buffers —
# checked on return; halt on a mismatch" — esp-idf's own Kconfig help) is
# the standard balance: STRONG/ALL instrument every function (measurable
# code-size and per-call overhead this target's 25+ fps render budget
# doesn't need to pay for); NONE means a stack-smashing bug corrupts
# memory silently instead of halting somewhere close to the actual fault
# — the opposite of "honest data" for a crash report. DRIFT-RULE WARNING
# (this file's own header, and PR #172): because the key is explicitly
# PRESENT (=y) on the maintainer's board, not absent, this default will
# NOT reach that board on its own — `idf.py reconfigure` alone is not
# enough. The maintainer must delete BOTH
# `CONFIG_COMPILER_STACK_CHECK_MODE_NONE` and its legacy-namespace mirror
# `CONFIG_STACK_CHECK_NONE` (this same choice is duplicated under both
# Kconfig prefixes on a generated sdkconfig — confirmed by reading the
# maintainer's file) from the board's sdkconfig, or `idf.py fullclean` +
# reconfigure it entirely, for this new default to actually take effect.
CONFIG_COMPILER_STACK_CHECK_MODE_NORM=y
